outputs.exact_statement:
For every three distinct active indices $i,j,k$, there are actual forms $B_{ijk}$ and $C_{ijk}$ with $$\label{eq:tensor-actual-boundaries}
 t_i t_j t_k U_t=D_t B_{ijk},\qquad
 b_i b_j b_k V=D C_{ijk}.$$ The first equality is over the full parameter algebra $T_p$.

The function $$\label{eq:edge-adjusted-potential}
 f_\tau(h)=\mathcal C(s(h,\tau))
                   +\tfrac{\epsilon}{2}\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}(u v(h,\tau))$$ has internal Jacobian ideal equal to the full curvature ideal of the affine slice. Here the internal derivatives are only the derivatives with respect to the $h_j$.

After fixing the evaluations on the extra letters and on all plus meridians, the direct sum of the two vertex degree-one spaces maps isomorphically, by difference of restrictions, to $\mathcal H$. Moreover, $$\label{eq:vertex-h2-isomorphism}
 H^2(A;\mathbb C)\oplus H^2(B;\mathbb C)
       \longrightarrow\bigoplus_i H^2(Y_i;\mathbb C)$$ is an isomorphism. In particular each vertex summand maps injectively.

At the joint origin the tangent images of $H_A$ and $H_B$, with the external parameters fixed, are complementary. For each vertex, $$\label{eq:vertex-square-and-gradient}
 S_P(H_P)\in I_P^2,
 \qquad (\partial_h S_P)(H_P)=M_P r_P,$$ where $M_P$ is an actual coefficient matrix whose reduction at the joint origin has full column rank $q_P$. Consequently the restricted gradient ideal equals $I_P$.
